---
import Math from "../../../components/Math.astro";
import { replayFault as study } from "../../../data/experiments";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "A replay-fault pilot that stopped | Independent Reinforcement Learning Lab";
const description =
  "One calibration checkpoint suggested persistent harm from missing replay rewards. Six predeclared DQN checkpoint forks did not reproduce a stable directional effect.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={study.slug}
  article
  published="2026-09-01"
  updated={study.updated}
>
  <article class="research-note">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>{study.kind.toUpperCase()} {study.number}</span>
        <span>01 SEPTEMBER 2026</span>
        <span>STOPPED AT GATE</span>
      </div>
      <h1>A replay-fault pilot <em>that stopped.</em></h1>
      <p class="article-deck">{study.subtitle}</p>
      <p class="article-standfirst">
        One excluded calibration root showed a 13.2% performance deficit after
        its faulty replay records had been evicted. Across six predeclared roots,
        only three deficits were positive and the median was 1.15%. The study
        did not meet its continuation rule.
      </p>
      <div class="article-byline">
        <span>Patrick Starvaggi</span>
        <span>4 minute read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <aside class="study-notice" aria-labelledby="study-status-title">
      <p id="study-status-title">PILOT CLOSED · 31 AUG 2026</p>
      <div>
        <strong>The rule required five positive pairs and median post-eviction debt of at least 10%. The observed values were three and 1.15%.</strong>
        <span>All six checkpoint pairs passed the sham, source-integrity, and learner-health checks.</span>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="question-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>01</span><p>THE QUESTION</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="question-title">Can a replay fault outlive its records?</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          A faulty target changes a network. That network supplies later bootstrap
          targets, so an error might continue to matter after the record that caused it
          has left replay.
        </p>
        <p>
          I tested that possibility with the compact reference DQN for MinAtar
          Breakout. After 500,000 training frames, each complete checkpoint was copied
          into clean and fault branches. The clean replay buffer was unchanged. In the
          fault branch, the 16 newest rewards equal to <Math tex="+1" /> were stored as
          <Math tex="0" />. Every subsequently collected reward was correct.
        </p>
        <p>
          Networks, optimizer state, environment state, replay position, and random
          states began identically. A clean/clean sham fork had to continue
          bit-identically before either treated pair could run. The modified records
          carried stable IDs, making every replay draw and the final eviction directly
          observable.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="protocol-grid">
        <div><dt>Benchmark</dt><dd>MinAtar Breakout</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Replay</dt><dd>Uniform FIFO · 100k</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Continuation</dt><dd>200k frames</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Evaluation</dt><dd>20 fixed episodes / 10k</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          For each training root, I integrated clean return minus fault return from the
          first evaluation after every altered record was gone through the fixed
          horizon. Dividing by clean performance over that interval produced the signed
          relative post-eviction deficit, <Math tex="D_{\mathrm{post}}" />. Positive
          values mean the fault branch performed worse.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="calibration-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>02</span><p>CALIBRATION</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="calibration-title">One root selected the fault size.</h2>
        <p>
          An excluded checkpoint tested fault sizes <Math tex="K\in\{4,8,16\}" />
          in order. The fixed rule was to lock the first size with at least 10%
          post-eviction deficit, or stop after 16. The six pilot roots and their
          evaluation panels had already been declared.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="exact-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Calibration fault-size screen" tabindex="0">
        <p class="table-label">EXCLUDED CALIBRATION ROOT</p>
        <table class="exact-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Altered rewards</th><th scope="col">Post-eviction deficit</th><th scope="col">Decision</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>9.13%</strong></td><td>Advance</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>8</strong></td><td><strong>9.66%</strong></td><td>Advance</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>16</strong></td><td><strong>13.16%</strong></td><td>Lock</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Sixteen altered rewards cleared the threshold. That result chose the pilot
          intervention; it did not establish an effect across independently trained
          agents.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section results-section" aria-labelledby="result-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>03</span><p>THE RESULT</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="result-title">The calibration signal did not reproduce consistently.</h2>
        <p class="result-lead">
          Three roots showed positive post-eviction deficits and three showed negative
          deficits. The median was 1.15%, well below the predeclared 10% threshold.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="results-figure">
        <img
          src={study.results.figure}
          alt="Six paired DQN deficit trajectories fluctuate around zero. Three root-level post-eviction deficits are positive and three are negative, with a median of 1.15 percent."
        />
        <figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Left: clean return minus fault return for every predeclared checkpoint pair. Right: cumulative relative deficit after all altered records were absent. The orange line marks 10%; the black dashed line is the observed median.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="trial-ledger" aria-label="Diagnostic pilot result">
        <div><strong>3 / 6</strong><span>Positive deficits</span></div>
        <div><strong>1.15%</strong><span>Median deficit</span></div>
        <div><strong>6 / 6</strong><span>Healthy clean runs</span></div>
        <div><strong>6 / 6</strong><span>Technical checks</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose result-summary">
        <p><strong>Decision:</strong> stop or redesign. The pilot does not authorize the planned exposure-versus-residency study.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>04</span><p>THE READING</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="reading-title">No stable directional effect.</h2>
        <p>
          The pilot does not prove that the exact effect is zero. Six roots cannot
          characterize the full training distribution. They are sufficient to reject
          the continuation rule written for this project: this fault, learner, task,
          and outcome did not produce a signal stable enough for the proposed mechanism
          study.
        </p>
        <p>
          The stop is empirical rather than technical. Every checkpoint learned above
          the fixed health threshold, every clean continuation remained healthy, and
          every exact sham passed. Enlarging the fault beyond the declared maximum,
          searching intervention times, or retaining favorable roots would answer a
          different question after seeing the result.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section reproducibility" aria-labelledby="record-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>05</span><p>THE RECORD</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="record-title">The result remains inspectable.</h2>
        <p>
          The compact record includes one row per independent training root, every
          unsmoothed paired evaluation, the machine-readable gate decision, and the
          figure. The repository contains the frozen protocol, declared roots,
          checkpoint-fork implementation, replay instrumentation, and analysis.
        </p>
        <p class="figure-downloads">
          <a href={study.results.pairMetrics} download>Root metrics (CSV)</a>
          <a href={study.results.trajectories} download>Paired evaluations (CSV)</a>
          <a href={study.results.summary} download>Gate record (JSON)</a>
          <a href={study.results.figure} download>Figure (SVG)</a>
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pstarvaggi/independent-rl-lab/tree/main/experiments/replay_fault">Open the protocol and code <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="article-closing">
      <p>
        One checkpoint supplied a plausible story. Six new checkpoints supplied a
        reason not to publish that story as a finding.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/experiments/">All studies →</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
